# Line endings.
#
# Every blob in this repo is already stored with LF - `git ls-files --eol` reports
# `i/lf` for all 233 tracked text files - so this file changes no committed content.
# `git add --renormalize .` against it is a no-op, and there is no formatting commit
# to add to .git-blame-ignore-revs.
#
# What it changes is the *working tree*. Without it, git falls back to each clone's
# `core.autocrlf`, which is `true` by default on Windows: LF blobs get checked out as
# CRLF. Prettier's `endOfLine` defaults to `"lf"`, so `npm run format:check` - and
# therefore `npm run verify` - fails on all 77 client files on a Windows clone while
# passing on a Linux CI runner, where autocrlf is off. The same command has to mean
# the same thing in both places or CI is not a gate, it is a second opinion.
#
# `eol=lf` also matches .editorconfig, which has declared `end_of_line = lf` for every
# file since before this file existed, and it stops a clone with `core.autocrlf=false`
# from committing CRLF blobs that would then fail CI for everyone else.
#
# An existing working tree does not re-convert on its own. Once per clone:
#
#     git rm --cached -r .
#     git reset --hard
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Not text, whatever the content heuristic decides. Never touch these.
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
